+  A span is what the transcript holds after the later of the owner's last message and the guard's last evaluated stop, whose place in the transcript the guard records: a push or a question answers the request of its own span, never a later one, however long the owner types nothing. The guard's record is one file and belongs to one session, whose id, from the hook's input, it holds beside the wait's text, the place (an offset in the transcript as read, one beyond the file's end reading as none), for each opener a fingerprint of the request line whose push it last found (a hash, never the line), what its last refusal was for, an opener owed and a question's mark; read in a session of another id (a resumed or forked one, a second session in the Workspace) it reads as empty, so a standing wait or request is announced once more there, and the owner's message after the recorded place empties the wait, the fingerprints and the mark, so the same wait set again after an answer is a new one and no mark outlives the owner's word. A request that stands while the work goes on is announced once: a line that is word for word one whose push the guard found needs no new push until the owner writes. A fingerprint is written only at a stop where that push stood in the span, never at a stop let through after a dropped debt, so a line that was never pushed is refused again at its next stop; rule 12 says that a standing request is repeated in its words. The guard reads the transcript as it reads a pending wake-up today, once more after two seconds where the call is not found, and allows where it cannot read. The run-sheet finding and these are gathered into one refusal. A refusal for a missing push leaves a debt in the guard's record, the opener owed, or either opener where a new wait's message held no line: the stop that follows, which the host marks as continued and the guard allows today, is refused once more only where the request still stands, that is, where its last message holds a `Needs you` line, of either opener, and the span since the refusal holds no push with that line's opener, or where either opener is owed, the new wait still stands and the message still holds no line; the debt is then dropped either way, so a request is refused twice at the most, a request withdrawn after the first refusal is not refused again, a line written in place of a wait that no longer stands is held like any other, and a push that was only late in the transcript is found. No other continued stop is let through unread, as every one is today, but the one that follows a debt's second refusal, which ends the chain. After a refusal of another kind (the phase check's, the header's, the agent timer's) the three rows are evaluated once; after one of these rows' own refusals that left no debt (the labels', or the run-sheet finding's gathered with them) the two push rows are, the labels and the run sheet not again; a refusal there leaves the usual debt, so a request first written in answer to any refusal is held too. A chain is four refusals at the most (another check's, the labels' or the run sheet's, a push row's, its debt's), under the eight after which the host ends one. The question form's check writes its own refusal into the same record with its place, and the first question the guard allows after it spends that mark, as the next evaluated stop does: the second attempt passes without the guard reading a refused call back from the transcript, and a later question of the same span is checked as the first was. The check runs before every question form of every session on the computer, so it allows a call outside a bridge Workspace, by the stop hook's own test, and wherever it cannot write its mark: no bound of the host ends a chain of refused calls, and the guard must not begin one. The refusal of a choice in prose says that a question about the software's features or behaviour stays a conversation in the owner's words (rule 12's bound, KP-075), so that it pushe